{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "52",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old para four obese Caucasian woman with a two-year history of lower abdominal pain was referred for further management of bilateral adnexal masses. A CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis showed a complex cystic lesion of 7–8 cm in the right adnexum and bilateral ureteric obstruction. Baseline blood tests, including hematology and tumor markers (CA 125 -26 IU/ml), were normal. Serum tests indicated normal liver function and moderately impaired renal function (creatinine 10–15, urea > 200). Her medical history includes obstructive sleep apnea, asthma, glaucoma, hypothyroidism, hypertensive cardiomyopathy, and bipolar disorder; she is registered disabled. She had a copper intrauterine contraceptive device in place for the past three years. Due to her body habitus (BMI 42), abdominal and pelvic examination was challenging and non-specific, with the only notable finding being bilateral adnexal fullness. She was managed within the gynecological oncology multidisciplinary framework (MDT). CT films revealed bilateral basal pleural thickening, bilateral hydronephrosis, retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy associated with retroperitoneal fibrosis, and bilateral adnexal cystic masses. A small volume of ascites was also noted. MRI was performed for further characterization of the adnexal masses. The right adnexal complex cystic lesion measured 8 × 7 × 7 cm in diameter with a thick, irregular wall (up to 2 cm) and several septations (3–4 mm). There were no enhancing papillary formations, although the wall and septa enhanced. The appearance suggested an ovarian lesion with possible involvement of the Fallopian tube. The left adnexal cyst measured 5 × 6 × 4 cm, had a smooth wall, and contained a single thin septation, consistent with a benign ovarian cyst. Both cysts contained fluid that was low on T1 and high on T2, with no evidence of fat or blood. Surrounding the cysts posteriorly, the fat appeared streaky, and there was marked thickening of the adjacent fascial planes, including the mesorectal fascia up to 1 cm. The fascial thickening extended up the pre-sacral space and along the posterior aspect of the pelvic sidewalls, causing ureteral obstruction. Enlarged lymph nodes (up to 1.8 cm in short axis) were present along both pelvic sidewalls, notable for their very high T2 signal intensity. Nodes were also observed in the inguinal and para-aortic regions. The MDT recommended clinical review and imaging-guided biopsy of lymph nodes.\n\nHistology revealed non-caseating granulomatous lymphadenitis with negative stains for acid-fast bacilli and fungi.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
